---
// Mandala: Accessible by design, built from the Figma frames "Mandala accessibility" (1680),
// "Mandala accessibility / 1280", "Mandala accessibility / 1024" and "Mandala accessibility / Mobile 320".
// The text zoom samples, the focused and disabled buttons and the criteria list are HTML,
// so they follow the theme and can be read by screen readers.
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Link from '../../components/Link.astro';
import CaseFigure from '../../components/case-study/CaseFigure.astro';
import { url } from '../../utils/url';
import '../../styles/case-study.css';
import hero from '../../assets/mandala-accessibility/hero.jpg';
import heroDark from '../../assets/mandala-accessibility/hero-dark.jpg';
import paletteTests from '../../assets/mandala-accessibility/palette-tests.png';

const zoomSamples = [
  { size: 'm', text: 'Sample text 100% zoom.' },
  { size: 'l', text: 'Sample text 200% zoom.' },
  { size: 'xl', text: 'Sample text 400% zoom.' },
];

const criteria = [
  '1.3.1 Info and Relationships',
  '1.4.3 Contrast (minimum)',
  '1.4.4 Resize text',
  '1.4.10 Reflow',
  '1.4.11 Non-text contrast',
  '1.4.12 Text spacing',
  '1.4.13 Content on hover or focus',
  '2.1.1 Keyboard',
  '2.3.3 Animation from interactions',
  '2.4.7 Focus visible',
  '2.5.8 Target size (minimum)',
  '4.1.2 Name, role, value',
  '4.1.3 Status messages',
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Mandala: Accessible by design"
  description="How accessibility is built into Mandala Design System: WCAG 2.2 Level AA as the floor, automated and manual tests for every component, and evidence for every claim."
>
  <article class="case-study">
    <div class="hero">
      <h1 class="hero__title">Mandala: Accessible by design</h1>
      <CaseFigure
        src={hero}
        srcDark={heroDark}
        alt="Four people stand side by side: a woman in a wheelchair, a blind man with a white cane and a guide dog, a woman, and a person with a prosthetic arm. Coloured pencils lie in the corner."
        width={1262}
        caption="Created with AI, so that no real person’s image is used without their consent."
        bleed
        eager
      />
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <div class="text">
        <p>
          Somewhere, right now, someone is trying to submit a support ticket with a screen reader, or fill in a form after a stroke has changed how they process visual
          information, or navigate a dashboard with a keyboard because a mouse is not an option today. They did not choose to need more from the software they are
          using. The software either meets them there, or it does not.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is the premise Mandala Design System is built on. Not as a compliance exercise bolted onto finished components, but as the starting condition for every
          decision that follows.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="floor">
      <h2 id="floor">A floor, not a ceiling</h2>
      <div class="text">
        <p>
          Every component in this system is built to WCAG 2.2 Level AA as a mandatory baseline. That is not a target I aim for and sometimes miss; it is the floor
          beneath which nothing ships.
        </p>
        <p>
          I did not build this on a single accessibility .md file. The whole of my accessibility course is built into the system, together with links to multiple
          resources that the AI reads before it builds anything.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="legal">
      <h2 id="legal">Meeting legal requirements</h2>
      <div class="text">
        <p>Building properly to that standard also means I am already meeting obligations that are no longer optional.</p>
        <p>
          Section 508 in the United States sets WCAG 2.0 Level AA as its bar. The European Accessibility Act relies on the European standard EN 301 549, which until
          now has pointed to WCAG 2.1 and was updated in 2026 to align with WCAG 2.2, with its formal EU recognition still to come.
        </p>
        <p>
          Because every WCAG 2.2 Level AA requirement builds on the earlier versions, a component that meets 2.2 also meets 2.0 and 2.1, which means the groundwork
          for compliance is already in place today under both, and Mandala already meets the version the European standard now uses. This is not the reason
          accessibility matters here, but it is a welcome consequence of doing the work properly in the first place, rather than treating compliance as a separate
          scramble once a product is already built.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="structural">
      <h2 id="structural">Structural, not aspirational</h2>
      <div class="image-pair">
        <CaseFigure
          src={paletteTests}
          lightBox
          alt="Two contrast tables for the blue palette, one for light mode and one for dark mode, blurred."
          width={549}
          caption="All colour palettes are tested for WCAG 2.2 accessibility compliance."
        />
        <CaseFigure alt="" width={560} caption="Text scales to meet WCAG requirements on full page zoom and on text only zoom.">
          <div class="zoom" role="img" aria-label="The same sample text at 100%, 200% and 400% zoom.">
            {zoomSamples.map((sample) => <span class:list={['zoom__sample', `zoom__sample--${sample.size}`]}>{sample.text}</span>)}
          </div>
        </CaseFigure>
      </div>
      <div class="text">
        <p>
          The difference between a design system that talks about accessibility and one that enforces it comes down to a simple test: what happens when someone
          forgets?
        </p>
        <p>In Mandala, the answer is that the system catches it before it ships.</p>
        <p>
          Every component carries its accessibility specification alongside its code, not as an afterthought written up later, and that specification records the full
          lists of both automated and manual tests performed on it.
        </p>
        <p>Both lists matter, and neither is a formality.</p>
        <p>
          Automated checks run on every story in Storybook, and static analysis flags unsafe patterns before a pull request is even reviewed, but automated testing
          covers only part of what accessibility asks of a component, and nowhere near all of it. For that reason every component also has a list of manual tests, as
          long as its automated one, that a human must perform before it can pass.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is a large part of what human-governed and AI-powered means in practice: a person performs as many tests as the AI does, because that is how
          accessibility works. Nothing is called finished until both lists are complete and everything passes, and every component clears three separate gates: the
          code itself, the manual testing, and a final sign-off that checks the work against every decision the team has already locked in.
        </p>
        <p>
          Wrong output should be structurally impossible, not just discouraged. That principle shapes the tooling as much as it shapes the components.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="people">
      <h2 id="people">Built for the people who rely on it most</h2>
      <div class="image-pair">
        <CaseFigure alt="" width={165} caption="The button receives an outline on focus to make it clearly visible to keyboard users.">
          <span class="focus-demo text-label" role="img" aria-label="A solid button labelled Button in focus state, with a clear outline around it.">Button in focus state</span>
        </CaseFigure>
        <CaseFigure
          alt=""
          width={271}
          caption="A disabled element stays in the keyboard navigation and gets a tooltip on hover and focus, explaining the reason for it being disabled, and what the user should do to enable it."
        >
          <span class="disabled-demo" role="img" aria-label="A grey disabled button with a dark tooltip above it that reads: Tooltip explaining why the button is disabled.">
            <span class="disabled-demo__tooltip text-legends">Tooltip explaining why the button is disabled.</span>
            <span class="disabled-demo__button text-label">Disabled button</span>
          </span>
        </CaseFigure>
      </div>
      <div class="text">
        <ul class="bullets">
          <li>Colour is never the only way I communicate meaning.</li>
          <li>Every interactive element has a name a screen reader can announce, whether or not it has a visible label.</li>
          <li>Focus is always visible, and it never fires an action just by landing somewhere.</li>
          <li>Disabled controls stay reachable and explain themselves, rather than vanishing from the tab order the way a native disabled attribute would.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          None of these are edge cases. They are the ordinary, daily experience of people who use assistive technology, and they are treated as such.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="verified">
      <h2 id="verified">Verified, not assumed</h2>
      <div class="row row--text-first">
        <CaseFigure alt="" width={614} boxWidth={648} caption="WCAG 2.2 guidelines tested for the button component.">
          <ul class="criteria text-quote-attribution">
            {criteria.map((criterion) => <li>{criterion}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </CaseFigure>
        <div class="row__text text">
          <p>
            Every accessibility claim in this system is backed by evidence, tagged against the specific WCAG criterion it satisfies. That evidence exists so it can be
            checked, questioned, and eventually compiled into a formal conformance report; accessibility that cannot be demonstrated is not much better than
            accessibility that was never considered.
          </p>
          <p>
            The Button shows what that looks like in practice. It was tested against 13 WCAG criteria: 10 through both automated and manual testing, 2 through
            automated testing only, and 1 through manual testing only. In total that meant 27 automated tests and 30 manual ones. On colour contrast alone, axe ran 468
            tests covering every contrast requirement across the 18 colour themes, and found 0 violations. Other components need even more tests.
          </p>
          <p>
            Having AI run these tests across all the colour themes, and carry out the other automated tests too, saved a huge amount of time. The time saving only
            works because of how the system was set up from the beginning: accessibility is built into the foundations, so issues are prevented and not caught during
            testing. The tests are there to prove that it holds.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="documented">
      <h2 id="documented">Documented alongside the code</h2>
      <div class="text">
        <p>
          Every component I ship carries its own accessibility specification, written alongside the code rather than after it. For every component it documents the
          keyboard behaviour, the focus behaviour and anything else someone needs to use it accessibly, so anyone reaching for it can see exactly how it behaves and
          why.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="living">
      <h2 id="living">Mandala: a living design system</h2>
      <div class="text">
        <p>
          This is a living system, and it will keep growing. What will not change is the standard it is held to, or the belief underneath it: that good design has
          never had to choose between looking right and working for everyone.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section--padded" aria-labelledby="next">
      <h2 id="next">Next in the Mandala case study</h2>
      <div><Link href={url('/mandala/colour/')}>Mandala: The colour story</Link></div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Shared layout is in src/styles/case-study.css; this is what only this page needs */

  /* Text zoom samples: Inter at 16, 32 and 64 in the 560 wide Figma frame, scaled with the box */
  .zoom {
    container-type: inline-size;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space_8);
    inline-size: 100%;
    max-inline-size: 35rem; /* 560 */
  }

  .zoom__sample {
    --sample-size: min(1rem, 100cqi / 35);
    padding: var(--space_8) var(--space_32);
    border: var(--border_s) solid var(--tiles_border);
    border-radius: var(--radius_xl);
    background: var(--contrast_background);
    font-family: 'Inter Sample', system-ui, sans-serif;
  }

  .zoom__sample--m {
    font-size: var(--sample-size);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .zoom__sample--l {
    font-size: calc(var(--sample-size) * 2);
    line-height: 1.4375;
  }

  .zoom__sample--xl {
    font-size: calc(var(--sample-size) * 4);
    line-height: 1.25;
  }

  /* The Tooltip component above a disabled Button, drawn as a picture */
  .disabled-demo {
    display: inline-flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: calc(var(--space_6) + var(--space_4));
  }

  .disabled-demo__tooltip {
    position: relative;
    padding: var(--space_8) var(--space_12);
    border-radius: var(--radius_s);
    background: var(--text_default);
    color: var(--text_label);
  }

  /* Decorative arrow, 12 by 6, pointing down at the button */
  .disabled-demo__tooltip::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset-block-start: 100%;
    inset-inline-start: var(--space_12);
    inline-size: var(--space_12);
    block-size: var(--space_6);
    background: var(--text_default);
    clip-path: polygon(0 0, 100% 0, 50% 100%);
  }

  .disabled-demo__button {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-block-size: 3rem;
    padding: var(--space_12) var(--space_24);
    border-radius: var(--radius_xl);
    background: var(--disabled);
    color: var(--text_label);
  }

  .criteria {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    inline-size: 100%;
    margin: 0;
    padding-inline-start: var(--space_24);
  }
</style>
